  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Black Quotidian has generated measurable scholarly uptake, with 10 OpenAlex citations and a field-normalized standing in the upper tier of its year. The citing record points to continuing academic interest in the book’s core concerns: African American history, newspapers, and the visual and cultural study of everyday life.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Matthew F. Delmont’s Black Quotidian: Everyday History in African-American Newspapers makes a focused contribution to scholarship on the Black press by showing how digitized African-American newspapers can illuminate everyday lives in the twentieth century. The book’s impact record is still relatively compact, but it already shows clear scholarly uptake: citations began appearing in 2021 and increased in 2023, indicating that the work is being taken up in current research rather than remaining only a retrospective reference.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citation landscape suggests that Black Quotidian is resonating most strongly in adjacent humanities and social-science conversations, especially those organized around photography and visual culture, race and American history, and related work on media and modernity. That pattern fits the book’s method and argument: it treats newspapers not simply as sources of facts, but as archives that helped circulate Black history and value ordinary Black life. The citing works are mostly book chapters, which is consistent with the book’s role as a source for interpretive and synthetic scholarship.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The record also shows a modest but meaningful reach beyond citations. OpenAIRE indexes the book in multiple repository instances, including one open version, which improves access. In addition, the work has a presence in library and reader-reception systems, including WorldCat holdings and a LibraryThing entry noting recognition as a finalist for an ACLS Open Access Book Prize and Arcadia Open Access Publishing Award. These signals do not substitute for citation evidence, but they do indicate discoverability and visibility in scholarly and reader communities.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, the evidence portrays a book with solid early academic traction and a clear intellectual niche. Its strongest impact is scholarly and interpretive: Black Quotidian is being used by other researchers who are working on race, visual culture, media history, and the historical value of ordinary Black experiences. The broader public-discourse record is limited in the available data, so the strongest story here is one of emerging and targeted academic influence.</p>",
  "narrative_source": "template"
